# Revisions to skip when running `git blame`.
#
# Bulk mechanical reformats only. A commit belongs here when it changed no
# behaviour and no meaning — otherwise blame stops telling the truth about
# who decided what.
#
# GitHub honours this file automatically in its blame view. Locally it is
# one-time opt-in per clone:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# Every entry must be a full 40-character SHA that exists on `main`. Git
# fails blame outright on a revision it cannot resolve, so a wrong or
# short SHA here is worse than no file at all.
#
# NOTE: `main` is squash-merge only (merge commits and rebase merges are
# both disabled, and branches auto-delete). A commit SHA from a feature
# branch therefore never lands on `main` and must not be listed here — the
# SHA to record is the squashed commit `main` actually gets. That is why
# the Prettier reformat below is registered by a follow-up PR opened after
# the merge rather than inside the PR that did the reformatting.

# Prettier adoption, ADR-065 (PR #67) — reformatted 111 files across src/,
# tests/, scripts/ and the root configs.
#
# Measured effect of this entry, so the trade is on the record:
#
#   reformatted code   4,923 -> 151 lines wrongly blamed on the reformat
#                      (4,772 lines of authorship restored)
#   new prose/config     278 ->  93 lines still correctly blamed on it
#                      (185 lines lose it)
#
# The loss is real and is the price of squash-merging. Because `main` takes
# one commit per PR, this SHA bundles the mechanical reformat with the
# Prettier config and the ADR that documents it, and --ignore-rev is
# all-or-nothing. For lines the commit genuinely authored, git looks for the
# most similar line in the parent and scatters attribution across unrelated
# history rather than admitting it has no answer — the ADR-065 block in
# decisions.md gets spread over about 25 old commits.
#
# Kept anyway: it is 26 lines of code recovered for every prose line lost,
# and the affected documents date themselves (every ADR carries a Date:, every
# PROGRESS.md entry is dated in its heading), so blame was never how you read
# them. If a future reformat can be landed on its own commit, prefer that.
